<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kumazawa Banzan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1619–1691</span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kumazawa Banzan was a Japanese Confucian scholar of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wang-yangming">Wang Yangming</a> school and a pupil of Nakae Tōju. In the service of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ikeda-mitsumasa">Ikeda Mitsumasa</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/okayama">Okayama</a> Domain he advised on government, flood control and famine relief, and helped shape the domain's reforms. His later writings criticised shogunal policy, and he spent his last years under house arrest at Koga.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kumazawa-banzan.jpg" alt="Kumazawa Banzan" width="320" height="317" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: photo by Reggaeman · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kumazawa_Banzan_portrait.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
